@Dell
The word is 'whoa'.
I don't know why this bothers me so much.

@Barry Ancona
Oddly, 'nottethered' has the same number of letters as SOFTHEARTED.
AMHIK!

@Some guy in

“If you aren’t happy with the puzzles the NYT is publishing, why don’t you spend ~tens of hours developing a crossword construction skill set and start submitting your own puzzles to the NYT, on the off chance that they might eventually accept and publish one of them at some point within the next year or so”.

I will never understand why some people continue to think that the old Uno reverse card is a good response to complaints about the easening of these puzzles.

@B

The link doesn't work, maybe because it's not Friday anymore.  Here's a live link (though the stats haven't changed much):

<a href="https://xwstats.com/puzzles/2026-10-24" target="_blank">https://xwstats.com/puzzles/2026-10-24</a>

@Paul Turner I’m doing the same, though at some point (probably when I noticed that the later-week puzzles were consistently more difficult - see Barry’s note) I started only doing Wed-Sat, so I won’t claim to have done the whole archive. I’m up to April 1999. 

The difference in difficulty level is striking. Putting aside then-contemporary references, the vocabulary is much more challenging, the clues more opaque… and there’s a lot more opera. A Friday or Saturday from that era takes me at least twice the time a modern one will. And I regularly have to google stuff, though many of those are the aforementioned opera and then-contemporary references (books and authors, mostly). But they’re a good workout. 

Today’s was a walk in the park in comparison.

@B
I started daily solving on the day after Christmas 2025, and have been working back through the archives. I'm back to April 2025 and have noticed no difference in difficulty yet.
When do other folks think the Easening started?

@nash.mark

I think we've been down this road before.  As I recall, it depends on how one defines an "ingredient".

It is true that yeast is often not added, but there's enough floating around in the air that it gets included in the flour and water just through our septic environment, along with other bacteria.

